--TEST--
`cancel` answers whether this call is what took the job out of the queue, rather than the state it left behind — `rule:concurrency/queue-four-members` and `rule:concurrency/claiming-is-one-statement`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Cancelling is a race the caller can lose, and losing it is the ordinary
// outcome rather than an unlucky one: § 4 lets a worker claim a pending job at
// any moment, so a cancel written late finds the work already running. A
// `void` member would make "cancelled it" and "too late" look identical at the
// call site, and throwing for the second would make that ordinary race an
// exception. So the answer is the one bit the caller cannot find out any other
// way.
var $receipt = Core\Queue::push("jobs/send-receipt.nvs");

bool $won = Core\Queue::cancel($receipt);

// And it is that bit, not the state the row ended up in — `status` is the
// member that answers a state, and asking one question of two members is how
// each stays about one thing.
Core\Queue\State $wrong = Core\Queue::cancel($receipt);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Queue\State`, found `bool`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
